# Security Validation Rule

## Purpose & Scope

Defines comprehensive security validation standards for application code. This rule ensures code follows security best practices, prevents common vulnerabilities, and protects against known attack vectors across multiple programming languages.

**Applies to**: All source code files before code review, merge, or deployment

**Does not apply to**: Configuration files, documentation, test data, or build artifacts

## Inputs (Contract)

- Source code files in supported programming languages
- Security scanning tools (Bandit, SonarQube, ESLint security plugins, etc.)
- OWASP Top 10 knowledge base
- Input validation libraries
- Authentication/authorization frameworks

## Outputs (Contract)

- Security vulnerability assessment
- OWASP Top 10 compliance report
- Input validation coverage report
- Authentication/authorization audit
- Data protection assessment
- Security validation checklist with specific issues and remediation steps

## Core Security Principles

**SECURITY IS NOT OPTIONAL - IT'S MANDATORY.**

**DEFENSE IN DEPTH - Multiple layers of security controls.**

**LEAST PRIVILEGE - Users and systems have minimum necessary permissions.**

**SECURITY BY DEFAULT - Secure configuration by default.**

## Mandatory Security Gates

### Gate 1: Input Validation and Sanitization (CRITICAL)

#### OWASP Top 10: A01 - Broken Access Control
- [ ] All user inputs validated and sanitized
- [ ] Parameterized queries used (SQL injection prevention)
- [ ] Proper output encoding (XSS prevention)
- [ ] Path traversal prevention
- [ ] Command injection prevention

#### OWASP Top 10: A02 - Cryptographic Failures
- [ ] No hardcoded credentials or secrets
- [ ] Proper encryption for sensitive data
- [ ] Secure random number generation
- [ ] Proper key management
- [ ] HTTPS/TLS for all communications

#### OWASP Top 10: A03 - Injection
- [ ] SQL injection prevention (ORM or parameterized queries)
- [ ] NoSQL injection prevention
- [ ] Command injection prevention
- [ ] LDAP injection prevention
- [ ] XPath injection prevention

#### OWASP Top 10: A04 - Insecure Design
- [ ] Proper authentication mechanisms
- [ ] Role-based access control
- [ ] Session management secure
- [ ] Proper error handling without information leakage
- [ ] Security headers implemented

### Gate 2: Authentication and Authorization (CRITICAL)

#### Authentication
- [ ] Multi-factor authentication where appropriate
- [ ] Secure password storage (bcrypt, argon2, PBKDF2)
- [ ] Password complexity requirements
- [ ] Account lockout after failed attempts
- [ ] Secure session management

#### Authorization
- [ ] Role-based access control (RBAC)
- [ ] Attribute-based access control (ABAC)
- [ ] Principle of least privilege
- [ ] Proper permission checks
- [ ] Audit logging for access control

#### Session Management
- [ ] Secure session tokens
- [ ] Session timeout implemented
- [ ] Session invalidation on logout
- [ ] Secure cookie settings
- [ ] Session fixation prevention

### Gate 3: Data Protection (CRITICAL)

#### Data Classification
- [ ] Sensitive data identified and classified
- [ ] Data encryption at rest and in transit
- [ ] Proper key management
- [ ] Data retention policies
- [ ] Data minimization principles

#### Privacy Protection
- [ ] GDPR compliance (if applicable)
- [ ] Data anonymization where appropriate
- [ ] User consent management
- [ ] Right to be forgotten implementation
- [ ] Data breach notification procedures

#### Logging and Monitoring
- [ ] Security event logging
- [ ] Audit trails for sensitive operations
- [ ] Real-time monitoring
- [ ] Alerting for suspicious activities
- [ ] Log retention policies

### Gate 4: Application Security (HIGHLY RECOMMENDED)

#### Code Security
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets or credentials
- [ ] Proper error handling without information leakage
- [ ] Security headers implemented
- [ ] Content Security Policy (CSP)
- [ ] Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) properly configured

#### Dependency Security
- [ ] Regular dependency updates
- [ ] Vulnerability scanning of dependencies
- [ ] No known vulnerable libraries
- [ ] Proper version pinning
- [ ] Supply chain security

#### Infrastructure Security
- [ ] Secure configuration management
- [ ] Network segmentation
- [ ] Proper firewall rules
- [ ] Secure deployment practices
- [ ] Infrastructure as Code security

